[36m(main_task pid=2959448)[0m Doc 3(Title: K-mer) are actually generated. This is due to read errors, but more importantly, just simple coverage holes that occur during sequencing. The problem is that these small fractions of the possible k-mers violate the key assumption of de Bruijn graphs that all the k-mer reads must overlap its adjoining k-mer in the genome by formula_5 (which can’t occur when all the possible k-mers aren’t present). The solution to this problem is to break these k-mer sized reads into smaller k-mer sized fragments in a process commonly called k-merization.[2] These short k-mers are inserted into a graph along with the original k-mers in a process known as (or generalization). The original k-mer reads are then deleted from the graph. These shorter k-mer fragments can also be very large, (size >= formula_10) up to the original k-mer read length (kmerLen = k), resulting in ""k-merization to k length."" This usage of k-merization may be due to issues for implementing the methods required to manipulate the desired range of k-mers or saving computational resources (also needed to manipulate the range of k-mers). For example: simply assigning higher values to formula_10 until the data is successfully assembled.[3] In this use, the option to choose the (formula_5) to reduce the amount of memory used in calculating the graphs. Thus, as stated before, reducing k-mer assembly can result in reduced memory utilization in computing but will add processing time in computing the de Bruijn graph.</information>
