[36m(main_task pid=2959448)[0m Doc 3(Title: "British Primitive goat") intervention. The British primitive goat encompasses what were previously considered four interbreeding landrace varieties: Their characteristics were similar enough to classify them together, though some sources have treated them separately, such as the UN Food and Agriculture Organization, which considers some of these strains extinct. The British primitive goat descends from goats introduced around 5,000 years ago, along with some sheep and cattle, to the British Isles by the first farmers in the Neolithic period, the era of Stonehenge. A small, all-weather animal, it provided these subsistence pastoralists some milk, and was invaluable for its meat, skin, hair and tallow.</information>
